    resLi1: "Latitude & Longitude — Your exact position in decimal degrees and DMS format",
    resLi2: "Accuracy — The estimated error radius. A lower number means higher precision",
    resLi3: "Altitude — Your height above sea level (WGS84 ellipsoid), when available",
    h2Tips: "Tips for Better GPS Accuracy",
    tipLi1: "Go outdoors with a clear view of the sky",
    tipLi2: "Wait 10-30 seconds for the GPS to lock on to more satellites",
    tipLi3: "Enable 'High Accuracy' mode in your device's location settings",
    tipLi4: "Avoid areas near tall buildings or dense tree cover",
    faqItems: [
      { question: 'How do I find my GPS coordinates?', answer: 'This tool detects your GPS coordinates <strong>automatically</strong> when the page loads — your browser will ask for location permission the first time. If detection fails or you deny permission, a button appears so you can retry manually.' },
      { question: 'What format are the GPS coordinates in?', answer: 'By default, coordinates are shown in <strong>decimal degrees</strong> (DD) format, which is the most widely used format for digital applications. You can convert to DMS, UTM, or other formats.' },
      { question: 'How accurate are the GPS coordinates?', answer: 'Accuracy depends on your device and environment. Modern smartphones provide 3-5 meter accuracy outdoors. Indoors, accuracy may drop to 10-50 meters. The accuracy radius is shown alongside your coordinates.' }
    ]
  },
  es: {
    title: "Buscador de Coordenadas GPS — Ubicación Exacta | MyLocationLab",
